INT. BAGGAGE CLAIM, MIDNIGHT TERMINAL - NIGHT

A carousel loops empty. Flickering departures board. The cleaners have gone home.

SABRINA OLUWAFEMI-STRAND (30s, courier, hard-shell case handcuffed to her wrist) waits at belt six.

Beside her, a tall man holds a paper cup: VICTOR "VIC" ANANDARAJAH-KELLER (40s, airport security, lanyard, deadpan).

At the end of the belt, a third figure examines the departures board: INSPECTOR MIRA TOLENTINO-ACHEBE (50s, plainclothes, press badge folded in her palm).

VIC
Ma'am, you've been here three hours.

SABRINA
My bag is delayed.

VIC
Which bag?

SABRINA
The blue one.

VIC
You don't have a blue one.

SABRINA
I'd like to speak to your supervisor.

VIC
I am my supervisor.

SABRINA
Then I'd like another one.

MIRA
(without turning)
Miss Oluwafemi-Strand.

SABRINA
(freezing)
Do I know you?

MIRA
Not yet. But I know the case on your wrist.

Sabrina's hand tightens on the handle.

SABRINA
It's personal.

MIRA
It's evidence.

VIC
(to Mira)
Evidence of what?

MIRA
Of a very bad weekend.

Sabrina whips the case up and swings it at Vic's head. He ducks. It clips Mira's shoulder. Mira stumbles back; Sabrina bolts for the exit, dragging the case on its chain.

VIC
Stop!

MIRA
Let her run.

VIC
Inspector?

MIRA
(calling)
Sabrina! The carousel's about to start!

Sabrina stops, turns. The belt shudders and begins to turn, empty. She looks at it, then at them.

SABRINA
What's that supposed to mean?

MIRA
(overlapping)
It means turn it over.

VIC
(overlapping)
It means she's cornered.

^

Sabrina stares. The case drops open on its chain, spilling nothing. A few sheets of paper flutter to the floor.

SABRINA
(whispering)
Oh.

MIRA
Yes.

SABRINA
It's empty.

MIRA
It was always empty.

Sabrina laughs, short and hollow. Slowly she sinks to the floor beside the belt. Vic takes off his lanyard and sets it beside her.

VIC
Tea?

SABRINA
Please.
